<KkShell
  title="AI Search Optimization (Preview)"
  description="Be the business ChatGPT, Google AI, and Perplexity name, in the Dead River look."
  kicker="AEO"
  headline="WHEN SOMEBODY ASKS CHATGPT WHO TO CALL, IT NAMES THREE BUSINESSES. BE ONE OF THEM."
  sub="More people now ask an AI assistant &ldquo;who should I call&rdquo; instead of scrolling ten blue links. The assistant picks. We make sure it picks you."
  faqTitle="AEO, STRAIGHT ANSWERS"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p class="kk-dear">Hey,</p>
  <p>Do me a favor. Open ChatGPT right now and ask it for the best business in your trade in your city.</p>
  <p>It'll name three. Maybe four. Go ahead, I'll wait.</p>
  <p>Were you one of them?</p>
  <p>If you weren't, here's what that means. Every person who asked that question this month never saw you. Not on page two. Not at all. The assistant made the shortlist, and you weren't on it.</p>
  <p>That's not a future problem. That's happening right now, and it's getting bigger every month. Google puts an AI answer on top of the results. Perplexity and Gemini skip the results entirely. People are asking a machine who to trust, and the machine is answering.</p>
  <h2>WHY IT NAMES THEM AND NOT YOU</h2>
  <p>These assistants don't rank pages the way Google does. They read a handful of sources they trust, pull out the facts, and write an answer.</p>
  <p>For a service business, those sources are your own site, your Google Business Profile, your reviews, the directories that list you, and anything written about you anywhere else.</p>
  <p>If those sources disagree with each other, or say nothing specific, the assistant names the competitor who made it easier. Your hours say one thing on Google and another on your site. Your service page says "quality you can trust" and nothing about price, timeline, or who it's for. There's no number the model can quote. So it quotes somebody else.</p>
  <p>Most service sites were built for Google in 2019. They bury the plain facts under slogans. AI models need the opposite: plain facts, clear structure, and proof they can repeat.</p>
  <h2>WHAT WE DO ABOUT IT</h2>
  <p>Six things. None of them are magic. All of them are work.</p>
  <p><strong>Entity and listing cleanup.</strong> Your name, address, phone, hours, services, and service area, identical on your site, Google Business Profile, Bing, Apple, and the directories the models read. Boring. Also the first thing that's wrong at almost every business we look at.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer-first pages.</strong> Each service page answers the question a buyer asks in the first paragraph, with the price range, the timeline, and who it's for. Not three paragraphs in.</p>
  <p><strong>Structured data.</strong> Organization, LocalBusiness, Service, FAQ, Article, and Review schema on every page, validated, so a model can read the facts without guessing.</p>
  <p><strong>Proof the model can quote.</strong> Case studies with real numbers and dates. Reviews with replies. A guarantee in writing. Models cite specifics, not claims.</p>
  <p><strong>Citations and mentions.</strong> Profiles and mentions on the directories, review sites, and local press the assistants already cite for your trade.</p>
  <p><strong>Monthly answer testing.</strong> We ask ChatGPT, Gemini, Perplexity, and Google AI Mode the questions your buyers ask, log who gets named, and fix what blocks you. Then we ask again next month.</p>
  <h2>WHAT IT'S NOT</h2>
  <p>It's not a replacement for SEO. It sits on top of it. A page still has to be indexed, fast, and clear for Google before an assistant will read it. If your foundation isn't there, we build that first.</p>
  <p>It's not overnight. The assistants refresh what they know on their own schedule. Most businesses see their first citations in 60 to 90 days. Anybody promising you'll be in ChatGPT next week is guessing.</p>
  <p>And it's not a trick. There's no secret tag. The businesses that get named are the ones with consistent facts and real proof, everywhere the models look. We just do that work so you don't have to.</p>
  <h2>HERE'S THE PART I CARE ABOUT</h2>
  <p>Getting named is only half of it. The person who asked still has to become a booked job. That's why we treat this as one of the channels inside Demand Flow, not a thing on its own. The assistant sends them, the page answers them, the phone rings, and somebody calls back inside five minutes. Same path, same guarantee.</p>
  <p>If you want to know which assistants name you today and which ones don't, that's the first thing we check on a call. Takes ten minutes, and it's a little uncomfortable to watch.</p>
  <p class="kk-ps">P.S. Part 2 of Marketing is F*cking Easy is about finding the people who are already looking. AI assistants are where a lot of them are looking now. Free, further down.</p>
</KkShell>
